  <!-- ── Nomenclature ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="cmm-nomenclature" aria-labelledby="cmm-nomenclature-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cmm-nomenclature-title">How a probe name is read</h2></div>
    </div>
    <p class="mgdb-section-intro">By Amarillo F, Koumbaris G and Bass HW \(2005\). A probe name states four things at once: which sorghum species the BAC came from, which maize marker selected it, where the FISH signal was seen, and which maize sequence was used to find it. Two naming rules are in use, one for core bin markers and one for every other mapped marker.</p>

    <div class="cmm-name-grid">
      <article class="mgdb-card cmm-name-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-ok">Core bin marker</span>
        <h3><a href="/data_center/fish?id=12098&amp;map=892372">spb-CBM9.02_S65 \(bz1\)</a></h3>
        <dl class="cmm-name-parts">
          <div>
            <dt><code>spb</code></dt>
            <dd><i><u>S</u>orghum <u>p</u>ropinquum</i> <u>B</u>AC. <code>sbb</code> in a name means <i><u>S</u>orghum <u>b</u>icolor</i> <u>B</u>AC instead.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>-CBM</code></dt>
            <dd>Cross-hybridizing at high stringency to the <u>C</u>ore <u>B</u>in <u>M</u>arker.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>9.02</code></dt>
            <dd>The <a href="/bin_viewer">core bin marker</a> for locus 9.2, as used in the UMC 1998 linkage map from <a href="/person?id=40191">G. Davis</a> and colleagues.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>_S65</code></dt>
            <dd>The <u>S</u>hort arm cytogenetic locus, <u>65</u>% of the distance from the centromere \(0%\) to the telomere \(100%\). Written elsewhere as 9S.65 or map position &minus;.65.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>\(bz1\)</code></dt>
            <dd>The maize DNA sequence, <i>bz1</i>, used to query the sorghum genome for a corresponding BAC clone.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="cmm-name-reading">So <code>spb-CBM9.02_S65 \(bz1\)</code> is a <i>Sorghum propinquum</i> BAC FISH probe with a high degree of sequence similarity to maize core bin marker 9.2, producing FISH signals at 9S.65 on pachytene-stage chromosome spreads.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="mgdb-card cmm-name-card">
        <span class="mgdb-pill mgdb-pill-info">Other mapped marker</span>
        <h3><a href="/data_center/fish?id=143330&amp;map=892372">spb-9.03_L04 \(rgpr3235a\)</a></h3>
        <dl class="cmm-name-parts">
          <div>
            <dt><code>spb</code></dt>
            <dd><i><u>S</u>orghum <u>p</u>ropinquum</i> <u>B</u>AC, as above.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>-9.03</code></dt>
            <dd>The maize genetic bin. The probe was selected using a maize marker sequence known to map inside linkage bin 9.03, somewhere between CBM9.03 and CBM9.04. The bins are from <a href="/data_center/reference?id=223825">Davis et al., 1999</a>.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>_L04</code></dt>
            <dd>The <u>L</u>ong arm cytogenetic locus, <u>4</u>% of the distance from the centromere \(0%\) to the telomere \(100%\). Written elsewhere as 9L.04 or map position .04.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>\(rgpr3235a\)</code></dt>
            <dd>The maize DNA sequence, <i>rpgr3235a</i>, used to query the sorghum genome for a corresponding BAC clone.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="cmm-name-reading">So <code>spb-9.03_L04 \(rgpr3235a\)</code> is a <i>Sorghum propinquum</i> BAC FISH probe similar to maize marker probe rgpr3235, whose maize marker lies in linkage bin 9.03 and whose FISH signals were detected at 9L.04.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-note">
      <strong>Cytogenetic positions are in centiMcClintocks.</strong> Each chromosome arm is one unit long, with the centromere at 0, and that length is divided into 100 <a href="/coordinateDef">centiMcClintocks</a>. A position of 9S.33 means discrete FISH signals a third of the way along the short arm of chromosome 9, measured from the centromere.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Probe selection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="cmm-probes" aria-labelledby="cmm-probes-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cmm-probes-title">How a probe was selected</h2></div>
    </div>
    <p class="mgdb-section-intro">Maize marker-selected sorghum BAC FISH probes were identified in several ways, depending on the method of detection and on the type of probe or query used. Any combination of the two is possible; these are the ones with a worked example in the map.</p>

    <div class="mgdb-table-scroll">
      <table class="mgdb-table cmm-probe-table">
        <caption class="mgdb-visually-hidden">Methods of detection, the probe or query type each uses, and an example probe</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Method of detection</th><th scope="col">Probe or query type</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Hybridization<span>to sorghum BAC filter grids</span></th>
            <td><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/AY772455" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFLP insert</a> core bin marker</td>
            <td><a href="/data_center/fish?id=41373&amp;map=892372">spb-CBM9.01_S79 \(umc109\)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Hybridization<span>to sorghum BAC filter grids</span></th>
            <td><a href="/data_center/overgo?id=746425">Overgo</a> designed to detect maize locus <a href="/data_center/locus?id=143330">rpgr3235</a></td>
            <td><a href="/data_center/fish?id=143330&amp;map=892372">spb-9.03_L04 \(rpg3235a\)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><i>In silico</i><span>BLAST or equivalent</span></th>
            <td>RFLP, <a href="/data_center/overgo">Overgo</a>, or any other mapped sequence</td>
            <td class="mgdb-muted">&mdash;</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Synteny<span>from sorghum BAC sequence data</span></th>
            <td class="mgdb-muted">&mdash;</td>
            <td><a href="/data_center/fish?id=12768&amp;map=892372">sbb-CBM9.03_S13 \(wx1\)</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── FISH methods ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="cmm-methods" aria-labelledby="cmm-methods-title">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="cmm-methods-title">FISH methods</h2></div>
    </div>
    <p class="mgdb-section-intro">As described by Koumbaris and Bass \(2003\), updated by <a href="/person?id=917944">Ferdie Amarillo</a> and <a href="/person?id=15943">Hank Bass</a>.</p>

    <ol class="cmm-steps">
      <li>DNA from the sorghum BAC was labelled <i>in situ</i> with digoxigenin, or directly with a fluorescent dCTP \(Alexa-fluor 546-OBEA-dCTP\), by random-primed labelling.</li>
      <li>The labelled DNA was used as a FISH probe on pachytene chromosome spreads from maize chromosome addition lines of oat.</li>
      <li>
        FISH was carried out with a three-colour probe mix:
        <ul>
          <li>maize total DNA direct-labelled with Alexa488-dUTP, painting the whole maize chromosome;</li>
          <li><a href="/data_center/marker?id=892612">MCCY</a>, a Cy-5 oligonucleotide probe homologous to the maize CentC repeat, marking the centromere;</li>
          <li>the sorghum digoxigenin-labelled BAC, detected by indirect immunofluorescence with commercial kits in which the FITC reagents were substituted with rhodamine reagents. Alexa546-labelled probes were detected directly, without secondary staining.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Images were collected with a digital camera and de-blurred by iterative 3D deconvolution.</li>
      <li>Chromosomes were straightened and measured computationally.</li>
      <li>Mean locations are reported as the fractional distance along a chromosome arm, in <a href="/coordinateDef">centiMcClintocks</a>.</li>
      <li>Sorghum BACs for use as FISH probes were found by online search: MaizeGDB, for the overgo probe used for each marker or locus, and the BACman utility programs at the University of Georgia.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="mgdb-note mgdb-note-info">
      <strong>The BACman utilities are gone.</strong> <code>chibba.agtec.uga.edu/duplication/bacman/</code>, named in step 7 of the published method, no longer answers. It is recorded here because it is part of how the map was made, not because it can still be used.
    </div>
  </section>

